(A) Pair-sum invariant and the AM-GM drift (PROVED; checks below are exhaustive in range)
   |T(n)-n| = ceil(n/2) for n <= 2e6: True;  T(2i-1)+T(2i) = 4i-1 = (2i-1)+2i for i <= 1e6: True
   3n-1 map: U(2i)+U(2i+1) = 4i+1, image pair {i, 3i+1}, i <= 1e6: True
   product ratio T(2i-1)T(2i)/((2i-1)2i) = 3/4 + 1/(8i-4) (exact, i < 5000): True
   pairs mapped onto themselves: T: i in [1] (the cycle {1,2});  U: i in [0] (pair {0,1}, both fixed)
   sum_(n<=2M) T(n) = sum_(n<=2M) n = M(2M+1): True
   odd q <= 99, sheets +-1: sum-preserving perfect matchings (odd n <-> even (q-2)n +- 1) exist only for [(3, 1), (3, -1)]
   (two evens or two odds can never share a pair: for q >= 3 both displacements then have the same sign;
    for q = 1 every displacement is <= 0; so a sum-preserving pair is odd n with the even m = (q-2)n +- 1)
   displacement over [1,2M] for (qn+1)/2, M = 500: (q, sum, (q-3)M^2/2) = [(1, -250000, -250000), (3, 0, 0), (5, 250000, 250000), (7, 500000, 500000), (9, 750000, 750000)]
   => for q != 3 the displacement grows like M^2, so no partition into zero-sum blocks of bounded diameter exists;
      (for q >= 5 unbounded 'Hilbert hotel' partitions do exist, see below, so the diameter bound is necessary).
