(d) The self-similar engine behind the proof (Gerbicz): if a + b = s^2 then (49a + c) + (49b - c) = (7s)^2
   base chain of [1,25]: [18, 7, 9, 16, 20, 5, 4, 21, 15, 10, 6, 19, 17, 8, 1, 3, 22, 14, 2, 23, 13, 12, 24, 25, 11]
   the 49 chains T(c) = (49 a_1 + c, 49 a_2 - c, ...), c = -24..24, are square-sum chains: True;
   together they partition [25, 49*25+24] = [25, 1249] exactly: True
   The remaining work of the proof is to glue the 49 chains with the 24 small numbers (Gerbicz's 'nice pairs');
   that step is not re-verified here.  This exact self-similarity is the macrocosm engine of the problem.

(e) Mechanism test 1: other target sets.  A vertex near n has about c*n^alpha partners.  If the transition
    is a degree threshold, the last exception n* should sit where c*n*^alpha reaches the same small value.
   squares           c = 0.414: last n without chain  24 (last degree obstruction  18); last n without loop  31 (last degree obstruction  30);  c*sqrt(n) at the loop threshold n = 32: 2.34; undecided chains [], loops [101]  (1s)
   triangular k>=2   c = 0.586: last n without chain   8 (last degree obstruction   8); last n without loop  14 (last degree obstruction  11);  c*sqrt(n) at the loop threshold n = 15: 2.27; undecided chains [], loops [103]  (1s)
   squares+2squares  c = 0.707: last n without chain  12 (last degree obstruction   9); last n without loop  20 (last degree obstruction  15);  c*sqrt(n) at the loop threshold n = 21: 3.24; undecided chains [], loops [75, 82, 87, 88, 95, 101, 114, 125, 126]  (5s)
   cubes (literature: Rivera's Puzzle 311 via OEIS A071984): first cubic loop at n = 473;
     c = 2^(1/3) - 1 = 0.260, alpha = 1/3: c*473^(1/3) = 2.03
   odd squares: a + b = 1 (mod 8) splits G_n into the residue pairs {0,1},{2,7},{3,6},{4,5} mod 8, so no chain
   exists for any n >= 8 (a congruence obstruction, not a density one): thinning the target by congruences
   disconnects the graph, which is why the density test uses unions and triangular numbers instead.

(f) Mechanism test 2: small cycles of the Collatz-type maps n -> (qn + s)/2 (n odd), n/2 (n even)
    For a cycle with a odd steps and K steps: n |2^K - q^a| = |B| <= K 2^(K-1) (q/2)^a; big cycles need 2^K ~ q^a.
