##### procgen_brackets_20260924_legendre.py
====================================================================================================
PART 4. Legendre-type statements for odd-square brackets
====================================================================================================

(a) brackets m = 1..30000 (odd squares up to 3.600e+09; 15.0s)
    min primes per bracket (m >= 2): 5 at m = 2;  per Legendre half: 2;  per Oppermann quarter (m >= 2): 1
    first 12 brackets, quarter counts: [[1, 1, 1, 1], [1, 1, 2, 1], [1, 1, 2, 2], [1, 2, 2, 2], [2, 1, 4, 1], [2, 2, 2, 3], [3, 2, 2, 2], [4, 2, 4, 3], [1, 4, 2, 4], [3, 3, 3, 4], [4, 3, 4, 3], [2, 4, 4, 5]]
    brackets with <= 6 primes: [(1, 4), (2, 5), (3, 6)]
    count / (4m/log(2m+1)): mean 1.0000, min 0.8835 (m >= 101)
    minimum over m of each quarter separately: [1, 1, 1, 1]; quarter minima for m >= 100: [16, 15, 16, 13]

(b) Brocard's conjecture as a statement about unions of brackets
    min primes in (p^2, p'^2] over consecutive odd primes p' <= 60001: 5 (p = 3, p' = 5, 1 bracket(s))
    twin primes p' = p + 2 give a single bracket, so '>= 4 primes per bracket' (Oppermann) => Brocard, while
    Legendre alone gives only >= 2 there.

(c) Interval lengths at x ~ (2m)^2: bracket 8m = 4 sqrt(x); Legendre interval 2 sqrt(x) + 1
    Baker-Harman-Pintz (2001): a prime in [x - x^0.525, x] for all large x (x_0 not explicit).  x^0.525 <= 4 sqrt(x)
    iff x <= 4^40 = 1.209e+24; beyond that BHP intervals are longer than brackets, so BHP never implies
    'a prime in every bracket'.  Under RH (Cramer) gaps are O(sqrt(x) log x): still a log too long.
    Known: Legendre verified to n^2 ~ 2e19 (maximal prime gaps; Oliveira e Silva-Herzog-Pardi 2014, per the
    Wikipedia summary, not read), hence >= 2 primes in every bracket below 2e19; exceptional-set bounds for
    Legendre: Bazzanella (Arch. Math. 75 (2000) 29-34), and O(N^eps) exceptions under Lindeloef (Bazzanella 2013).
    Cube analogue PROVED: Ingham (1937) primes between large consecutive cubes; Dudek (2016) explicit.

(d) Negative control: Collatz statistics per bracket against prime counts (m <= 1000, n <= 4.0e6)
    corr(detrended mean total stopping time of B_m, detrended normalised prime count of B_m), 11 <= m <= 1000: -0.0056  (|r| < 0.064 is noise)
    No provable link is known: bracket statements see only archimedean size and primality, T sees 2-adic digits;
    the pairing family of part 2 keeps every bracket and every prime fixed while changing tree-ness.
